1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:02,338
On the night of March second,

2
00:00:02,358 --> 00:00:05,380
twenty twenty four, armed men attacked the biggest prison

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:07,071
in Haiti. The National Penitentiary,

4
00:00:07,091 --> 00:00:09,437
in the heart of Port au Prince.

5
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:13,117
The next day, prison officials held a roll call.

6
00:00:13,137 --> 00:00:16,098
The prison had held about three thousand seven hundred

7
00:00:16,118 --> 00:00:18,747
men. According to a Haitian human rights group,

8
00:00:18,767 --> 00:00:19,741
ninety nine answered.

9
00:00:21,061 --> 00:00:23,513
That same weekend, a second prison fell.

10
00:00:23,533 --> 00:00:25,278
According to the Associated Press,

11
00:00:25,298 --> 00:00:28,456
more than four thousand seven hundred prisoners walked free

12
00:00:28,476 --> 00:00:30,222
in two days. Within weeks,

13
00:00:30,242 --> 00:00:33,047
the prime minister could not even fly home.

14
00:00:33,067 --> 00:00:34,106
And he resigned.

15
00:00:34,826 --> 00:00:36,793
Two and a half years later,

16
00:00:36,813 --> 00:00:39,110
the story took a turn nobody expected.

17
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:41,428
El Salvador, the country that built CECOT,

18
00:00:41,448 --> 00:00:43,084
sent its soldiers into Haiti.

19
00:00:43,104 --> 00:00:45,401
How did a country lose its prisons,

20
00:00:45,421 --> 00:00:47,388
its capital, and almost its government,

21
00:00:47,408 --> 00:00:48,381
in one weekend?

22
00:00:50,121 --> 00:00:52,900
Chapter 1. A country without a president.

23
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,143
On July seventh, twenty twenty one,

24
00:00:57,163 --> 00:01:00,027
gunmen broke into the home of Haiti's president,

25
00:01:00,047 --> 00:01:02,910
Jovenel Moïse, and killed him in his bedroom.

26
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:06,517
Haiti had no elected president after that.

27
00:01:06,537 --> 00:01:10,256
A neurosurgeon named Ariel Henry became acting prime minister,

28
00:01:10,276 --> 00:01:13,163
and ran the country without an election.

29
00:01:13,183 --> 00:01:15,655
Parliament's terms expired. One by one,

30
00:01:15,675 --> 00:01:17,732
Haiti's elected institutions emptied out.

31
00:01:18,452 --> 00:01:20,555
Into that vacuum stepped the gangs.

32
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:22,325
By early twenty twenty four,

33
00:01:22,345 --> 00:01:24,095
according to the Associated Press,

34
00:01:24,115 --> 00:01:26,926
gangs controlled around eighty percent of the capital,

35
00:01:26,946 --> 00:01:27,988
Port au Prince.

36
00:01:28,708 --> 00:01:31,950
Facing them was a national police force of around

37
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:35,212
nine thousand officers, for a country of more than

38
00:01:35,232 --> 00:01:37,386
eleven million people. Many were outgunned.

39
00:01:37,406 --> 00:01:39,198
Many had already been killed.

40
00:01:39,918 --> 00:01:41,621
And then there were the prisons.

41
00:01:41,641 --> 00:01:44,206
Human rights groups had warned for years that Haiti's

42
00:01:44,226 --> 00:01:46,790
prisons were among the most overcrowded in the world.

43
00:01:46,810 --> 00:01:49,375
Most of the people held in them had never

44
00:01:49,395 --> 00:01:51,959
been convicted. They were waiting for trials that might

45
00:01:51,979 --> 00:01:52,534
never come.

46
00:01:53,254 --> 00:01:55,999
The biggest of all was the National Penitentiary,

47
00:01:56,019 --> 00:01:57,727
in downtown Port au Prince,

48
00:01:57,747 --> 00:02:00,838
not far from the site of the presidential palace.

49
00:02:00,858 --> 00:02:02,566
In March twenty twenty four,

50
00:02:02,586 --> 00:02:04,640
according to a Haitian lawyers' group,

51
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:07,750
it held three thousand six hundred and ninety six

52
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:08,096
men.

53
00:02:08,816 --> 00:02:10,412
Among them were gang members.

54
00:02:10,432 --> 00:02:12,675
And men accused of killing the president.

55
00:02:14,215 --> 00:02:15,903
Chapter 2. Barbecue.

56
00:02:18,003 --> 00:02:19,982
To understand what happened next,

57
00:02:20,002 --> 00:02:22,382
you need to know one man.

58
00:02:22,402 --> 00:02:24,381
Jimmy Chérizier, known as Barbecue.

59
00:02:25,101 --> 00:02:27,181
Chérizier is a former police officer.

60
00:02:27,201 --> 00:02:30,332
He was fired from the force in twenty eighteen.

61
00:02:30,352 --> 00:02:33,482
Human rights groups have accused him of leading a

62
00:02:33,502 --> 00:02:36,282
massacre in the La Saline neighborhood that year.

63
00:02:36,302 --> 00:02:39,432
A Haitian human rights network counted seventy one dead.

64
00:02:39,452 --> 00:02:40,482
He denies it.

65
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:43,895
In twenty twenty, he united several gangs into an

66
00:02:43,915 --> 00:02:45,402
alliance called the G nine.

67
00:02:45,422 --> 00:02:48,115
The United States and the United Nations put him

68
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:48,718
under sanctions.

69
00:02:49,438 --> 00:02:52,320
At the end of February twenty twenty four,

70
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:54,497
prime minister Henry left the country.

71
00:02:54,517 --> 00:02:55,948
He traveled to Kenya,

72
00:02:55,968 --> 00:02:57,400
to finalize a deal.

73
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:00,665
Kenya would send one thousand police officers to lead

74
00:03:00,685 --> 00:03:02,842
an international mission against the gangs.

75
00:03:03,562 --> 00:03:05,189
On March first, in Nairobi,

76
00:03:05,209 --> 00:03:08,153
reporters asked Henry whether it was safe to go

77
00:03:08,173 --> 00:03:10,129
home. According to the Associated Press,

78
00:03:10,149 --> 00:03:10,788
he shrugged.

79
00:03:11,508 --> 00:03:13,088
On February twenty ninth,

80
00:03:13,108 --> 00:03:15,089
Chérizier announced a new alliance.

81
00:03:15,109 --> 00:03:18,689
Rival gangs, including the G nine and its enemies,

82
00:03:18,709 --> 00:03:20,290
would now work together.

83
00:03:20,310 --> 00:03:22,290
They called it Viv Ansanm.

84
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:23,090
Live together.

85
00:03:23,810 --> 00:03:25,373
Its goal, Chérizier said,

86
00:03:25,393 --> 00:03:26,956
was to topple Henry.

87
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:30,517
Within hours, gangs attacked police stations across the capital.

88
00:03:30,537 --> 00:03:32,100
Police officers were killed.

89
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,870
And then they went for the prisons.

90
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:38,029
Chapter 3. The night.

91
00:03:40,129 --> 00:03:41,911
On the night of Saturday,

92
00:03:41,931 --> 00:03:44,794
March second, armed gangs attacked the National Penitentiary.

93
00:03:44,814 --> 00:03:48,038
There was a gunbattle with police outside the walls.

94
00:03:48,058 --> 00:03:49,840
Then the gangs got in.

95
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:53,573
Inside, thousands of men saw the doors open.

96
00:03:53,593 --> 00:03:54,710
And they ran.

97
00:03:56,030 --> 00:03:57,434
By the next morning,

98
00:03:57,454 --> 00:03:59,215
the prison was almost empty.

99
00:03:59,235 --> 00:04:00,996
According to the lawyers' group,

100
00:04:01,016 --> 00:04:04,202
three thousand five hundred and ninety seven prisoners had

101
00:04:04,222 --> 00:04:05,982
escaped. About ninety seven percent.

102
00:04:06,002 --> 00:04:08,832
A UN source put the number slightly lower,

103
00:04:08,852 --> 00:04:10,969
at around three thousand five hundred.

104
00:04:10,989 --> 00:04:12,037
Nobody could check.

105
00:04:12,757 --> 00:04:15,831
Reporters who reached the prison found the doors open,

106
00:04:15,851 --> 00:04:17,893
and bodies outside. At least ten,

107
00:04:17,913 --> 00:04:19,611
according to early press reports.

108
00:04:20,331 --> 00:04:22,711
And it was not the only prison.

109
00:04:22,731 --> 00:04:25,796
Gangs also attacked the prison in Croix des Bouquets,

110
00:04:25,816 --> 00:04:27,167
east of the capital,

111
00:04:27,187 --> 00:04:28,538
the country's second largest.

112
00:04:28,558 --> 00:04:30,252
Hundreds more walked out there.

113
00:04:30,972 --> 00:04:33,507
In total, according to the Associated Press,

114
00:04:33,527 --> 00:04:36,793
more than four thousand seven hundred prisoners escaped that

115
00:04:36,813 --> 00:04:39,348
weekend. About a dozen people were killed.

116
00:04:40,068 --> 00:04:42,621
It was one of the largest prison breaks in

117
00:04:42,641 --> 00:04:44,909
modern history. And it happened in one weekend,

118
00:04:44,929 --> 00:04:47,482
in two prisons, in a country that no longer

119
00:04:47,502 --> 00:04:49,198
had a functioning government to respond.

120
00:04:50,718 --> 00:04:52,383
For comparison, El Salvador's CECOT,

121
00:04:52,403 --> 00:04:54,405
the largest prison in the Americas,

122
00:04:54,425 --> 00:04:56,765
was built to hold forty thousand men.

123
00:04:56,785 --> 00:04:59,798
In one weekend, Haiti lost the equivalent of more

124
00:04:59,818 --> 00:05:01,483
than a tenth of it,

125
00:05:01,503 --> 00:05:02,832
from just two prisons.

126
00:05:03,772 --> 00:05:06,121
Chapter 4. The men who stayed.

127
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:09,188
Not everyone ran.

128
00:05:10,508 --> 00:05:13,956
Among the few prisoners who stayed in the National

129
00:05:13,976 --> 00:05:16,654
Penitentiary were around eighteen former Colombian soldiers,

130
00:05:16,674 --> 00:05:20,122
accused of taking part in the assassination of President

131
00:05:20,142 --> 00:05:20,508
Moïse.

132
00:05:21,228 --> 00:05:23,060
They had good reason to stay.

133
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,530
If they walked out into gang controlled streets,

134
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:26,765
they might not survive.

135
00:05:26,785 --> 00:05:29,235
One of them recorded a video from inside,

136
00:05:29,255 --> 00:05:30,162
begging for help.

137
00:05:30,882 --> 00:05:33,973
They are massacring people indiscriminately inside the cells,

138
00:05:33,993 --> 00:05:34,751
he said.

139
00:05:35,471 --> 00:05:38,431
Colombia's foreign ministry asked Haiti to give the men

140
00:05:38,451 --> 00:05:41,080
special protection. They stayed in the empty prison,

141
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:44,061
waiting to see who would come through the door

142
00:05:44,081 --> 00:05:44,392
next.

143
00:05:45,112 --> 00:05:47,334
Most of the other inmates were gone.

144
00:05:47,354 --> 00:05:48,615
Some were gang members,

145
00:05:48,635 --> 00:05:50,536
back in their territory within hours.

146
00:05:50,556 --> 00:05:53,418
Some were men who had never been convicted of

147
00:05:53,438 --> 00:05:56,301
anything, and had simply been waiting for a trial.

148
00:05:56,321 --> 00:05:57,902
Nobody knew which was which.

149
00:05:59,442 --> 00:06:01,269
Chapter 5. Collapse.

150
00:06:03,369 --> 00:06:06,394
On March third, the government declared a seventy two

151
00:06:06,414 --> 00:06:09,100
hour state of emergency and a nighttime curfew.

152
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:11,129
It was extended for a month.

153
00:06:11,849 --> 00:06:13,457
It made little difference.

154
00:06:13,477 --> 00:06:17,119
On March fourth, gangs attacked Haiti's main international airport.

155
00:06:17,139 --> 00:06:19,153
Flights stopped. Prime minister Henry,

156
00:06:19,173 --> 00:06:21,595
still abroad, could not get home.

157
00:06:21,615 --> 00:06:24,443
He ended up stuck in Puerto Rico.

158
00:06:25,163 --> 00:06:26,484
Chérizier issued a warning.

159
00:06:26,504 --> 00:06:28,161
If Henry did not go,

160
00:06:28,181 --> 00:06:31,178
he said, it would lead directly to a civil

161
00:06:31,198 --> 00:06:33,190
war, which would end in genocide.

162
00:06:33,910 --> 00:06:36,946
On March eleventh, Henry announced he would resign,

163
00:06:36,966 --> 00:06:39,621
once a transitional council was in place.

164
00:06:39,641 --> 00:06:41,913
He formally stepped down in April.

165
00:06:41,933 --> 00:06:44,969
A presidential council of Haitian politicians took over.

166
00:06:45,689 --> 00:06:48,785
Meanwhile, the escapees were back on the streets.

167
00:06:48,805 --> 00:06:52,290
One of them, a gang leader named Ernst Julme,

168
00:06:52,310 --> 00:06:55,016
was killed by police later that month.

169
00:06:55,036 --> 00:06:58,521
Another, Dimitri Hérard, the former head of the presidential

170
00:06:58,541 --> 00:07:02,026
palace guard who had been jailed over the president's

171
00:07:02,046 --> 00:07:05,531
assassination, also escaped. He was later placed under U.S.

172
00:07:05,551 --> 00:07:05,921
sanctions.

173
00:07:06,641 --> 00:07:08,116
And on March fourteenth,

174
00:07:08,136 --> 00:07:10,359
the empty National Penitentiary caught fire.

175
00:07:10,379 --> 00:07:13,724
According to a human rights monitor quoted by the

176
00:07:13,744 --> 00:07:17,089
Miami Herald, it appeared to start in the maximum

177
00:07:17,109 --> 00:07:19,706
security section, which inmates called the Titanic.

178
00:07:19,726 --> 00:07:21,949
The cause was never made public.

179
00:07:23,489 --> 00:07:25,722
Chapter 6. Not the first time.

180
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:29,776
Haiti had seen mass escapes before.

181
00:07:30,496 --> 00:07:32,547
On January twelfth, two thousand ten,

182
00:07:32,567 --> 00:07:34,964
a massive earthquake struck Port au Prince.

183
00:07:34,984 --> 00:07:38,070
Estimates of the dead range from about one hundred

184
00:07:38,090 --> 00:07:40,487
thousand to more than two hundred thousand.

185
00:07:40,507 --> 00:07:42,213
The National Penitentiary's walls cracked,

186
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:45,319
and thousands of prisoners walked out into the ruined

187
00:07:45,339 --> 00:07:45,665
city.

188
00:07:46,385 --> 00:07:47,693
In August twenty fourteen,

189
00:07:47,713 --> 00:07:50,682
more than three hundred prisoners escaped from the prison

190
00:07:50,702 --> 00:07:52,010
in Croix des Bouquets.

191
00:07:52,030 --> 00:07:54,999
A month later, according to reports at the time,

192
00:07:55,019 --> 00:07:57,988
only about seventy five of them had been recaptured.

193
00:07:58,708 --> 00:08:01,665
Each time, the state rebuilt the walls.

194
00:08:01,685 --> 00:08:05,493
Each time, the system behind the walls stayed the

195
00:08:05,513 --> 00:08:08,895
same. Overcrowded. Underfunded. And in twenty twenty four,

196
00:08:08,915 --> 00:08:09,746
finally, defenseless.

197
00:08:11,286 --> 00:08:12,916
Chapter 7. The mission.

198
00:08:15,016 --> 00:08:18,105
The world's answer was the mission Henry had flown

199
00:08:18,125 --> 00:08:21,214
to Kenya for. Back in October twenty twenty three,

200
00:08:21,234 --> 00:08:23,633
the UN Security Council had approved it.

201
00:08:23,653 --> 00:08:26,051
A multinational force, led by Kenyan police.

202
00:08:26,771 --> 00:08:29,917
The first four hundred Kenyan officers landed in Port

203
00:08:29,937 --> 00:08:32,027
au Prince on June twenty fifth,

204
00:08:32,047 --> 00:08:35,193
twenty twenty four. The United States sent armored vehicles.

205
00:08:35,913 --> 00:08:37,296
Chérizier was not impressed.

206
00:08:37,316 --> 00:08:39,050
In June twenty twenty four,

207
00:08:39,070 --> 00:08:42,206
he said the Kenyans were there to protect the

208
00:08:42,226 --> 00:08:43,609
interests of American oligarchs.

209
00:08:43,629 --> 00:08:46,766
Later, he told a journalist that the foreign forces

210
00:08:46,786 --> 00:08:49,572
had not come to solve the country's problems.

211
00:08:50,292 --> 00:08:52,328
But the mission was always short.

212
00:08:52,348 --> 00:08:54,727
Short of people, and short of money.

213
00:08:54,747 --> 00:08:56,098
According to the UN,

214
00:08:56,118 --> 00:08:59,183
of seventy eight million dollars pledged to its trust

215
00:08:59,203 --> 00:09:02,267
fund, only around eleven million had actually arrived by

216
00:09:02,287 --> 00:09:03,638
March twenty twenty four.

217
00:09:04,358 --> 00:09:06,194
In January twenty twenty five,

218
00:09:06,214 --> 00:09:09,536
soldiers and military police from Guatemala and El Salvador

219
00:09:09,556 --> 00:09:11,021
arrived to join it.

220
00:09:11,041 --> 00:09:12,878
But the gangs kept expanding.

221
00:09:12,898 --> 00:09:14,734
In June twenty twenty five,

222
00:09:14,754 --> 00:09:18,076
Kenya's own president admitted the mission had made little

223
00:09:18,096 --> 00:09:18,447
progress.

224
00:09:19,167 --> 00:09:21,227
The violence spread beyond the capital.

225
00:09:21,247 --> 00:09:22,960
In October twenty twenty four,

226
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:25,386
gunmen attacked the town of Pont Sondé,

227
00:09:25,406 --> 00:09:27,120
north of Port au Prince.

228
00:09:27,140 --> 00:09:30,240
At least one hundred and fifteen people were killed,

229
00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:31,626
according to later counts.

230
00:09:31,646 --> 00:09:34,746
In December, in the Wharf Jérémie area of the

231
00:09:34,766 --> 00:09:37,866
capital, nearly two hundred people were killed in a

232
00:09:37,886 --> 00:09:40,292
single massacre, according to the United Nations.

233
00:09:40,312 --> 00:09:42,372
Many of the victims were elderly.

234
00:09:43,092 --> 00:09:45,577
The numbers from the UN tell the story.

235
00:09:45,597 --> 00:09:48,395
In the first three months of twenty twenty five,

236
00:09:48,415 --> 00:09:51,213
more than one thousand six hundred people were killed

237
00:09:51,233 --> 00:09:53,405
in Haiti. In the next three months,

238
00:09:53,425 --> 00:09:55,283
more than one thousand five hundred.

239
00:09:55,303 --> 00:09:58,101
By September, around one point four million people had

240
00:09:58,121 --> 00:09:59,667
been forced from their homes.

241
00:10:00,387 --> 00:10:01,706
Between July and September,

242
00:10:01,726 --> 00:10:04,718
at least one thousand two hundred and forty seven

243
00:10:04,738 --> 00:10:06,057
more people were killed.

244
00:10:06,077 --> 00:10:07,730
In July twenty twenty five,

245
00:10:07,750 --> 00:10:10,743
a senior UN official warned the Security Council that

246
00:10:10,763 --> 00:10:13,755
a total collapse of state presence in the capital

247
00:10:13,775 --> 00:10:15,763
could become a very real scenario.

248
00:10:16,483 --> 00:10:18,455
In May twenty twenty five,

249
00:10:18,475 --> 00:10:20,845
the United States designated Viv Ansanm,

250
00:10:20,865 --> 00:10:23,235
Chérizier's coalition, a foreign terrorist organization.

251
00:10:23,255 --> 00:10:25,226
In August, it charged Chérizier,

252
00:10:25,246 --> 00:10:28,811
and offered a reward of five million dollars for

253
00:10:28,831 --> 00:10:30,803
information leading to his arrest.

254
00:10:31,743 --> 00:10:34,302
Chapter 8. The gang suppression force.

255
00:10:36,402 --> 00:10:38,852
In the fall of twenty twenty five,

256
00:10:38,872 --> 00:10:42,028
the UN Security Council replaced the struggling mission with

257
00:10:42,048 --> 00:10:44,146
something bigger. A Gang Suppression Force,

258
00:10:44,166 --> 00:10:46,616
planned at five thousand five hundred personnel,

259
00:10:46,636 --> 00:10:48,028
with a stronger mandate.

260
00:10:48,748 --> 00:10:50,765
In April twenty twenty six,

261
00:10:50,785 --> 00:10:53,209
the first troops from Chad arrived.

262
00:10:53,229 --> 00:10:56,875
In January, security forces had destroyed three of Chérizier's

263
00:10:56,895 --> 00:10:58,504
homes in an operation.

264
00:10:58,524 --> 00:10:59,319
He survived.

265
00:11:00,039 --> 00:11:01,395
And Croix des Bouquets,

266
00:11:01,415 --> 00:11:04,491
the town whose prison had been emptied in March

267
00:11:04,511 --> 00:11:06,555
twenty twenty four, was still contested.

268
00:11:06,575 --> 00:11:08,275
Gangs were still active there.

269
00:11:08,995 --> 00:11:11,466
That is where, in October twenty twenty six,

270
00:11:11,486 --> 00:11:12,400
the story turned.

271
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:16,757
Chapter 9. Bukele's soldiers.

272
00:11:18,857 --> 00:11:21,162
On October eighteenth, twenty twenty six,

273
00:11:21,182 --> 00:11:23,487
four aviation technicians from El Salvador,

274
00:11:23,507 --> 00:11:25,425
serving with the international force,

275
00:11:25,445 --> 00:11:26,975
were ambushed in Santo,

276
00:11:26,995 --> 00:11:28,525
in Croix des Bouquets.

277
00:11:28,545 --> 00:11:31,238
They had been returning from a supermarket.

278
00:11:31,258 --> 00:11:34,339
Haiti's government later said they had been abducted.

279
00:11:35,059 --> 00:11:36,979
El Salvador's president, Nayib Bukele,

280
00:11:36,999 --> 00:11:38,531
the man behind CECOT,

281
00:11:38,551 --> 00:11:41,635
responded fast. He sent special forces to Haiti.

282
00:11:41,655 --> 00:11:44,740
Then many more. Reports of the total vary,

283
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:48,232
from around one thousand soldiers to as many as

284
00:11:48,252 --> 00:11:50,948
two thousand five hundred by early November.

285
00:11:51,668 --> 00:11:54,003
Bukele's own war on gangs is famous.

286
00:11:54,023 --> 00:11:55,685
Since March twenty twenty two,

287
00:11:55,705 --> 00:11:58,713
El Salvador has lived under a state of exception.

288
00:11:58,733 --> 00:12:01,404
More than eighty thousand people have been arrested.

289
00:12:01,424 --> 00:12:04,432
The government says it turned one of the most

290
00:12:04,452 --> 00:12:07,459
violent countries in the world into one of the

291
00:12:07,479 --> 00:12:08,805
safest in the region.

292
00:12:08,825 --> 00:12:11,832
Human rights groups have documented abuses and deaths in

293
00:12:11,852 --> 00:12:12,169
custody.

294
00:12:12,889 --> 00:12:15,919
Now those methods, or at least those soldiers,

295
00:12:15,939 --> 00:12:18,969
were in Haiti. Bukele publicly promoted the operation.

296
00:12:18,989 --> 00:12:22,401
Critics asked what exactly the Salvadoran troops were authorized

297
00:12:22,421 --> 00:12:24,307
to do, in another country.

298
00:12:25,027 --> 00:12:28,068
According to reports, most of the Salvadoran troops operate

299
00:12:28,088 --> 00:12:29,768
outside the UN backed framework.

300
00:12:29,788 --> 00:12:32,829
That has raised questions about their rules of engagement,

301
00:12:32,849 --> 00:12:34,529
and who they answer to.

302
00:12:35,249 --> 00:12:38,560
According to reports, an operation on November eighth left

303
00:12:38,580 --> 00:12:41,520
around twenty people dead in Croix des Bouquets.

304
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:44,851
On November tenth, Salvadoran forces reportedly destroyed the headquarters

305
00:12:44,871 --> 00:12:47,442
of a gang called four hundred Mawozo,

306
00:12:47,462 --> 00:12:49,662
and the home of its leader.

307
00:12:49,682 --> 00:12:52,623
The gang's leader has denied holding the Salvadorans.

308
00:12:53,343 --> 00:12:54,781
As of our latest check,

309
00:12:54,801 --> 00:12:57,405
there has been no public confirmation that the four

310
00:12:57,425 --> 00:12:58,571
technicians have been found.

311
00:13:00,091 --> 00:13:02,731
Two and a half years after Haiti's prisons were

312
00:13:02,751 --> 00:13:05,390
emptied, the country that became famous for locking up

313
00:13:05,410 --> 00:13:08,050
its gangs is now fighting gangs in the town

314
00:13:08,070 --> 00:13:09,823
where one of those prisons stood.

315
00:13:11,363 --> 00:13:13,179
Chapter 10. Where are they now?

316
00:13:15,279 --> 00:13:17,813
So what happened to the four thousand seven hundred?

317
00:13:18,533 --> 00:13:21,324
The honest answer is that nobody has published a

318
00:13:21,344 --> 00:13:22,886
full count. Some were killed.

319
00:13:22,906 --> 00:13:24,760
Some were recaptured, or came back.

320
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:27,571
Some went back to the gangs they came from.

321
00:13:27,591 --> 00:13:29,445
Many may simply have gone home,

322
00:13:29,465 --> 00:13:32,256
to wait for trials that were never going to

323
00:13:32,276 --> 00:13:32,880
happen anyway.

324
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:35,291
Haiti's largest prison stood empty,

325
00:13:35,311 --> 00:13:38,370
and then burned. The state that was supposed to

326
00:13:38,390 --> 00:13:39,738
run it nearly disappeared.

327
00:13:39,758 --> 00:13:42,817
And the gangs that opened its doors are now

328
00:13:42,837 --> 00:13:44,527
facing foreign soldiers from Kenya,

329
00:13:44,547 --> 00:13:46,238
Chad, Guatemala, and El Salvador.

330
00:13:46,958 --> 00:13:48,757
Will Bukele's methods work in Haiti?

331
00:13:48,777 --> 00:13:51,486
Or is Haiti too far gone for any outside

332
00:13:51,506 --> 00:13:54,216
force to fix? Tell us what you think in

333
00:13:54,236 --> 00:13:56,945
the comments. And to see the prison Bukele built

334
00:13:56,965 --> 00:13:58,158
for his own gangs,

335
00:13:58,178 --> 00:13:59,674
watch our story about CECOT,

336
00:13:59,694 --> 00:14:00,584
on this channel.
